== 6. The Example of the World

#strong[The sixth #emph[device] that Satan has to keep souls away from religious services is,]

By presenting before them the examples#footnote[John 7:48, 49. 1 Cor 1:26, 27. Mic 7:2, 3, 4.] of the greatest part of the world, who walk in the ways of their own hearts and who make light and slight of the ways of the Lord. “Why,” says Satan, “do you not see that the great and the rich, the noble and the honorable, the learned and the wise, indeed the greatest number of men, never trouble themselves about such and such ways? Why then should you be odd and fastidious? You would do far better to do as most do,” etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider those Scriptures that speak directly against following the sinful examples of men, such as that in Exodus: “You shall not#footnote[The way to hell is broad and well beaten; the way to be undone forever is to do as most do. (#emph[Argumentum turpissimum est turba]) the multitude is the weakest and worst argument, says Seneca.] follow a multitude in wrongdoing. When you testify in a lawsuit, do not pervert justice by siding with the multitude.” The multitude are generally ignorant and do not know the way of the Lord; therefore they speak evil of what they do not know. They are envious and maliciously set against the service and way of God, and therefore they cannot speak well of the ways of God. “This way is everywhere spoken against,” they said. So in Num 16: “Separate yourselves from them, and come out from among them.” So the apostle: “Have no fellowship with the fruitless deeds of darkness.” So Solomon: “Do not set foot#footnote[Prov 4:14; 9:6.] on the path of the wicked”; “Leave the foolish behind, and you will live.” Those who walk with the most will perish with the most. Those who do as the most do will before long suffer with the most; those who live as the most live must die with the most, and go to hell with the most.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that if you#super[+] will sin with the multitude, all the angels in heaven and men on earth cannot keep you#super[+] from suffering#footnote[Sin and punishment are linked together with chains of adamant. Of sin we may say what Isidore says of the serpent (#emph[Tot dolores, quot colores]): so many colors, so many dolors \[sorrows\].] with the multitude. If you#super[+] will be wicked with them, you#super[+] must unavoidably be miserable with them. Say to your soul, “O my soul, if you will sin with the multitude, you must be shut out of heaven with the multitude; you must be cast down to hell with the multitude,” etc. “Then I heard another voice from heaven say: ‘Come out of her, My people, so that you#super[+] will not share in her sins or contract any of her plagues.’” Come out in affection, in action, and in habitation; otherwise the infection of sin will bring on you#super[+] the infliction of punishment. So says the wise man: “He who walks with the wise will become wise, but the companion of fools will be destroyed,” or, as the Hebrew#footnote[ירוע רוע #emph[a Jeroange,] from #emph[ruange,] to be evil.] has it, will be broken in pieces. Multitudes may help you into sin; indeed, one may draw you into sin; but it is not multitudes that can help you to escape punishment. You#super[+] may see this in Moses and Aaron, who were provoked to sin by the multitude, yet were shut out of the pleasant land and fell by a hand of justice as well as others.

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider the worth and excellence of your immortal soul. Your soul is a jewel worth more#footnote[What wise man would fetch gold out of a fiery crucible, and risk his immortal soul (to gain the world) by following a multitude in those steps that lead to the chambers of death and darkness?] than heaven and earth. The loss of your soul is incomparable, irreparable, and irrecoverable; if that is lost, all is lost, and you are undone forever. Is it madness and folly for a man to kill himself for company? And is it not greater madness or folly to break the neck of your soul, and to damn it for company? Suspect that way in which you see multitudes walking, the multitude being a stream that you must row hard against, or you will be carried into that gulf out of which angels cannot deliver you. Is it not better to walk in a narrow way alone than to wander into crooked ways with company? Surely it is better to go to heaven alone than to hell with company. I might add other things, but these may suffice for the present; and I am afraid that if these arguments do not stir you#super[+], other arguments will work little on you#super[+].
